# Ignore rules for this repository. The base rules come first, then one block
# per ecosystem the project uses: Rust, then shell.
#
# .gitignore only applies to files git is not ALREADY tracking. Adding a line
# does not untrack what is already in. Check with: git ls-files

# -- secrets. Never committed, no exceptions ---------------------------------
# Listed BEFORE the files exist, so one can never be staged by accident.
# A credential file added here after the fact was trackable in between.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*
!.dev.vars.example
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
*.keystore
id_rsa
id_ed25519
credentials.json
service-account*.json
*.token
.*_token
.*-token

# -- scratch and temporary work ---------------------------------------------
.tmp/
tmp/
scratch/
.scratch/

# -- logs --------------------------------------------------------------------
*.log
logs/

# -- local databases ---------------------------------------------------------
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.sqlite-journal
*.sqlite-wal
*.db-shm
*.db-wal

# -- coverage and test output -----------------------------------------------
coverage/
.coverage
htmlcov/
test-results/
.nyc_output/

# -- agent and assistant local files ----------------------------------------
.claude/settings.local.json
CLAUDE.local.md
.aider*
.cursorignore

# -- reference material cloned for study ------------------------------------
# NOT IGNORED, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION. These rules used to read
# `references/`, on the reasoning that a tree is re-clonable from the URL its
# write-up records and only the derived file needs tracking.
#
# That reasoning was wrong twice over, and both were paid for. A sweep that
# kept only its conclusions left the next session re-cloning eleven trees to
# check one citation. A sweep that kept its data in an ignored directory lost
# it entirely, along with the tools it had written to gather it, because
# nothing untracked survives a session. A conclusion nobody can re-check is
# an opinion. docs/methodology/research.md section 3.4 is the rule.
#
# Where a corpus is genuinely too large for every clone, put it on its own
# BRANCH and say so in the write-up. That is still tracked. An ignore rule is
# not a size decision, it is a deletion nobody notices.
temp/

# WINDOWS RESERVED DEVICE NAMES. Two different things create one as a real
# file, and only the first is something you wrote:
#   1. your own redirect: 2>/dev/null under a shell that does not map it makes
#      a real file called nul, which git then tracks, which breaks git stash
#      outright, and which cannot be deleted by rm or by Python;
#   2. a tool's own argument list: podman machine ssh passes
#      -o UserKnownHostsFile=NUL to its ssh, and under Git Bash that is a
#      filename, so a 99-byte NUL holding an ssh host key lands in whatever
#      directory the command ran in. Usually a repository.
# MSYS_NO_PATHCONV does not prevent the second one: the argument never looked
# like a path. Ignoring the whole set is what stops the recurrence.
nul
CON
PRN
AUX
NUL
COM[1-9]
LPT[1-9]
CON.*
PRN.*
AUX.*
NUL.*
COM[1-9].*
LPT[1-9].*

# -- Rust --------------------------------------------------------------------
/target/
**/*.rs.bk
*.pdb

# Cargo.lock IS committed for a binary, because it is what makes a build
# reproducible. It is conventionally NOT committed for a library. Decide once,
# say which this is, and hold to it.

# Criterion benchmark output. The RUNNERS are tracked source; the CAPTURES are
# not. A run that IS the evidence for an item goes in deliberately:
#   git add -f bench/the-one-that-matters.json
/criterion/

# -- shell -------------------------------------------------------------------
# shellcheck and shfmt leave nothing behind, so this block is mostly about the
# things a shell project accumulates around itself.
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
.shellcheckrc.local

# A script that writes a report writes it here. The RUNNERS are tracked source;
# the CAPTURES are not. A run that IS the evidence for an item goes in
# deliberately with: git add -f
out/
reports/
